<main>
<section class="slide active" data-title="Start" id="slide-start">
  <div class="card hero">
    <div class="kicker">Student Lesson</div>
    <h2>Learning Law with GenAI: From Study Tool to Professional Judgment</h2>
    <p>This lesson teaches you how to use generative AI in ways that build legal expertise rather than substitute for it. It also connects your academic choices to professional responsibility: competence, confidentiality, candor, verification, supervision, and compliance with court-specific rules.</p>
    <p><strong>Estimated time:</strong> 80-100 minutes. <strong>Required output:</strong> completion and quiz-grade report downloaded as a PDF and submitted to Canvas.</p>
    <div class="note warning"><strong>Important:</strong> This lesson saves your work in this browser and creates a local report for Canvas submission. Your instructor may use the report for completion, participation, or a low-stakes grade.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Student information</h3>
    <p class="mini">This information appears only in the report generated in this browser.</p>
    <label for="studentName">Student name</label>
    <input id="studentName" type="text" placeholder="First and last name" oninput="state.studentName=this.value; saveState(); updateReportPreview();">
    <label for="courseName">Course / section</label>
    <input id="courseName" type="text" placeholder="Example: GenAI and the Practice of Law" oninput="state.courseName=this.value; saveState(); updateReportPreview();">
    <label for="studentId">Student ID or Canvas name (optional)</label>
    <input id="studentId" type="text" placeholder="Optional" oninput="state.studentId=this.value; saveState(); updateReportPreview();">
    <div class="choice-list"><label><input id="certStart" type="checkbox" onchange="state.certStart=this.checked; markActivity('start-cert', this.checked); saveState(); updateReportPreview();"> I understand that my report will certify my own completion and quiz responses.</label></div>
    <div class="btnrow"><button class="primary" onclick="go(1)">Start lesson</button></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="slide" data-title="1. Learning Paradox" id="slide-paradox">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kicker">Module 1</div>
    <h2>The paradox: the same tool can train you or hollow you out</h2>
    <p>GenAI can make you more capable when it creates practice, feedback, retrieval, and reflection. The same tool can also create an illusion of competence when it explains, organizes, drafts, or reasons so fluently that you feel finished before doing the cognitive work.</p>
    <p>Law school is a particularly dangerous environment for passive GenAI use because legal competence is not merely knowing rules. You must recognize which rules matter, apply them to new facts, distinguish strong from weak arguments, tolerate ambiguity, and construct analysis under time pressure.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Working rule:</strong> Good GenAI use should increase the amount of legal thinking you do. Risky GenAI use decreases that thinking while producing a polished artifact.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Interaction 1: Diagnostic poll</h3>
    <p>Choose the statement closest to your current study habit. This is not graded, but it appears in your report.</p>
    <div id="diagnosticPoll"></div>
    <div id="diagnosticFeedback" class="feedback"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>The first cognitive move rule</h3>
    <p>Use this rule before you decide whether GenAI is helping you learn or quietly taking over the work: <strong>Who made the first legal move?</strong></p>
    <p>If <strong>you</strong> first read, reason, write, argue, issue-spot, or commit to an answer, GenAI can often serve as a coach. If GenAI first briefs, drafts, decides, supplies the rule application, or gives you the model answer, you are probably skipping the work that builds legal competence.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Student version:</strong> You do the first legal move. GenAI may critique, question, organize, or generate more practice after that. You keep final judgment.</div>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Color</th><th>Clear student rule</th><th>Safe examples</th><th>Required safeguard</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="badge green">Green</span></td><td>You have already done the legal thinking, and GenAI is coaching or creating practice.</td><td>You write a rough holding, then ask GenAI to challenge vague language; you write an answer, then request critique; you ask for a hypo but require GenAI to withhold analysis until you answer.</td><td>Verify feedback against the case, notes, statutes, or assigned materials. You decide what is right.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge red">Red</span></td><td>GenAI does the competence-building work before you try.</td><td>GenAI briefs the case before you read it; GenAI drafts a graded memo; GenAI writes a practice-answer model before you attempt the answer.</td><td>Reverse the sequence: read/write/reason first, then use GenAI only for critique or additional practice.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge yellow">Yellow</span></td><td>The use may be legitimate, but the answer depends on permission, disclosure, confidentiality, scope, or timing.</td><td>Grammar cleanup after you write the full analysis; study-group use; accessibility or translation support; turning your own notes into a review sheet.</td><td>Check the syllabus or governing policy, disclose if required, and keep GenAI from changing substance unless that use is expressly allowed.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="note warning"><strong>How to handle yellow-light overlap:</strong> Do not treat “depends” as a trick answer. Turn it into a concrete question: <em>What fact changes the analysis?</em> Usually the fact is permission, disclosure, timing, confidentiality, or whether GenAI changed substance rather than presentation.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>The 4-D habit: delegation, description, discernment, diligence</h3>
    <p>When a GenAI use feels uncertain, run through these four questions. They work in law school and later in practice.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Question to ask yourself</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>Example of a strong answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Delegation</strong></td><td>What exactly am I letting GenAI do?</td><td>You must know whether you are delegating practice support or delegating the legal reasoning itself.</td><td>“I used GenAI to generate new hypotheticals, not to answer them for me.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Description</strong></td><td>Can I accurately describe the tool, task, and scope of help?</td><td>Disclosure is impossible if you cannot say what happened.</td><td>“I used ChatGPT to suggest counterarguments after I wrote my draft; I did not use it to draft the analysis.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Discernment</strong></td><td>Can I tell whether the output is wrong, incomplete, biased, or too confident?</td><td>LLMs can sound authoritative even when they hallucinate, omit nuance, or reproduce assumptions.</td><td>“I checked the rule against the assigned case and noticed the model overstated the holding.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Diligence</strong></td><td>What verification, permission, confidentiality, or citation check still has to happen?</td><td>Responsible use is not finished when the output appears. You still owe careful follow-through.</td><td>“Before submitting, I checked the syllabus, verified every citation, and removed confidential facts from prompts.”</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Clear examples before practice</h3>
    <p>The examples below are intentionally direct. Later in the lesson, you will see harder scenarios, but these give you the baseline.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Use pattern</th><th>Color</th><th>Why</th><th>Best student move</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>After reading a case, you write a rough holding and ask GenAI to question vague parts.</td><td><span class="badge green">Green</span></td><td>You made the first legal move by reading and extracting the holding. GenAI is acting as a critic.</td><td>Revise the holding, then verify it against the opinion and class notes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Before reading, you ask GenAI for the case brief and study only that summary.</td><td><span class="badge red">Red</span></td><td>GenAI performed the case-reading and principle-extraction work.</td><td>Read the case first, write a rough brief, then use GenAI only to test omissions or confusion.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your study group debates a problem first, then asks GenAI for counterarguments you may have missed.</td><td><span class="badge yellow">Yellow, possibly green with safeguards</span></td><td>The group did the first reasoning move, but collaboration and AI rules still matter.</td><td>Check the collaboration policy, document the use if required, and write your own analysis independently.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You ask GenAI to draft a graded memo, then edit the wording.</td><td><span class="badge red">Red</span></td><td>GenAI created the submitted legal analysis. Editing wording does not make the reasoning yours.</td><td>Write the legal analysis yourself. Use GenAI only for allowed proofreading, organization feedback, or critique after drafting.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You use GenAI for grammar cleanup after writing the full analysis.</td><td><span class="badge yellow">Yellow, often allowed if limited</span></td><td>Grammar support may affect presentation only, but policies vary and some require disclosure.</td><td>Check the policy, disclose if required, and prevent the tool from rewriting substance or changing legal reasoning.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You ask GenAI to generate three assault/battery hypos but withhold the analysis until after you answer.</td><td><span class="badge green">Green</span></td><td>GenAI creates practice opportunities, but you still issue-spot and analyze first.</td><td>Answer first, then ask for critique, missed issues, and one harder follow-up hypo.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Interaction 2: Safeguard clinic</h3>
    <p>For each scenario, choose the concrete safeguard that makes the use responsible or the repair that fixes the problem. You are not being asked to guess a label.</p>
    <div id="competenceCompass"></div>
    <div class="btnrow"><button class="secondary" onclick="checkCompassClinic()">Check safeguards</button></div>
    <div id="competenceCompassFeedback" class="feedback"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Checkpoint quiz 1: Learning or illusion?</h3>
    <div id="checkpoint1"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Explain-it-back sentence</h3>
    <p>Complete this sentence in your own words: <strong>“GenAI helps me learn when ____, but it prevents learning when ____.”</strong></p>
    <textarea id="explainBack1" oninput="state.reflections.explainBack1=this.value; markActivity('explain-back-1', this.value.trim().length>=90); saveState(); updateReportPreview();"></textarea>
    <p class="mini">A meaningful answer of at least 90 characters marks this interaction complete.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="btnrow"><button class="ghost" onclick="go(0)">Back</button><button class="primary" onclick="completeSlideAndGo(1,2)">Continue</button></div>
</section>

<section class="slide" data-title="2. Law School Learning" id="slide-learning">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kicker">Module 2</div>
    <h2>Why law school learning is different</h2>
    <p>You may have entered law school with study habits that worked in undergraduate courses: reading, highlighting, memorizing, and reproducing information. Those habits help with the first layer of legal learning, but they do not produce exam-ready legal analysis by themselves.</p>
    <p>In law school, memorizing the rule is usually the starting point, not the endpoint. A Torts exam does not merely ask for the elements of negligence. It asks whether you can identify every plausible claim and defense in a new fact pattern, select the relevant doctrine, generate arguments for both sides, and make calibrated conclusions under time pressure.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Old academic habit</th><th>Why it feels effective</th><th>Why it fails in law school</th><th>Better GenAI-supported habit</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Rereading explanations</td><td>Recognition feels like mastery.</td><td>Exams require recall and application.</td><td>Ask for practice questions after you attempt your own explanation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Collecting outlines</td><td>The final document looks organized.</td><td>The organization process is what builds the mental map.</td><td>Draft your own outline, then use GenAI to test gaps and inconsistencies.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reading model answers first</td><td>The model answer makes the issue seem obvious.</td><td>You did not practice spotting the issue yourself.</td><td>Write first; then ask GenAI for critique.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Seeking the answer</td><td>Certainty lowers anxiety.</td><td>Many legal questions require competing arguments.</td><td>Ask GenAI to pressure-test both interpretations you generated.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Interaction 3: Right-answer trap</h3>
    <p>Read the short situation. Choose the strongest learning move.</p>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Situation:</strong> A student asks, “What is the answer to this personal jurisdiction hypo?” GenAI gives a confident answer. The student copies the conclusion into notes.</div>
    <div id="rightAnswerTrap" class="big-choices"></div>
    <div class="btnrow"><button class="secondary" onclick="checkSingleChoice('rightAnswerTrap','Ask GenAI to critique the student’s own analysis after the student first identifies contacts, relatedness, and fairness factors.','rightAnswerTrapFeedback','The better move requires the student to generate the analysis first. GenAI becomes a critic, not an answer key.')">Check</button></div>
    <div id="rightAnswerTrapFeedback" class="feedback"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Interaction 4: Productive struggle plan</h3>
    <p>Write a two-step study rule you could use to prevent yourself from checking GenAI too early.</p>
    <textarea id="productiveStruggle" oninput="state.reflections.productiveStruggle=this.value; markActivity('productive-struggle', this.value.trim().length>=90); saveState(); updateReportPreview();"></textarea>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Checkpoint quiz 2: Legal learning mechanics</h3>
    <div id="checkpoint2"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="btnrow"><button class="ghost" onclick="go(1)">Back</button><button class="primary" onclick="completeSlideAndGo(2,3)">Continue</button></div>
</section>

<section class="slide" data-title="3. Surface & Structure" id="slide-structure">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kicker">Module 3</div>
    <h2>Surface features, deep structure, and the competence illusion</h2>
    <p>Novices often remember cases by their vivid facts. Experts see the legal pattern underneath the facts. A student who remembers “the hairy hand case” has surface familiarity. A student who can use that case to analyze expectancy damages, reliance damages, and the remedial goal of contract law is moving toward deep structure.</p>
    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="note"><strong>Surface feature</strong><br>Specific facts, names, events, objects, or narrative details.</div>
      <div class="note success"><strong>Deep structure</strong><br>The transferable legal framework: issue, rule, reasoning pattern, and policy tradeoff.</div>
      <div class="note warning"><strong>Competence illusion</strong><br>The feeling that you understand because an explanation made sense while you read it.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Interaction 5: Flip cards</h3>
    <p>Click each card to reveal what a novice might see and what an expert tries to extract.</p>
    <div class="flipgrid">
      <div class="flip" onclick="this.classList.toggle('flipped')"><div class="front"><span class="badge">Palsgraf</span><h3>The scale falls after fireworks explode.</h3><p class="mini">Click to reveal the deeper structure.</p></div><div class="back"><strong>Deep structure:</strong> duty, foreseeable plaintiff, proximate cause, and competing ways to limit liability.</div></div>
      <div class="flip" onclick="this.classList.toggle('flipped')"><div class="front"><span class="badge">Hadley</span><h3>The mill shaft arrives late.</h3><p class="mini">Click to reveal the deeper structure.</p></div><div class="back"><strong>Deep structure:</strong> foreseeability of consequential damages and information available at contracting.</div></div>
      <div class="flip" onclick="this.classList.toggle('flipped')"><div class="front"><span class="badge">International Shoe</span><h3>A company sells through salespeople in a state.</h3><p class="mini">Click to reveal the deeper structure.</p></div><div class="back"><strong>Deep structure:</strong> minimum contacts, relatedness, purposeful availment, and fair play/substantial justice.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="btnrow"><button class="secondary" onclick="markActivity('flip-cards',true); setFeedback('flipFeedback','Marked complete. The key is not remembering the facts alone; it is extracting the transferable structure.','good')">I reviewed the cards</button></div>
    <div id="flipFeedback" class="feedback"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Interaction 6: Surface or structure?</h3>
    <p>Use the large buttons to classify each statement. This replaces the earlier dropdown format.</p>
    <div id="surfaceStructureSet"></div>
    <div class="btnrow"><button class="secondary" onclick="checkDecisionSet('surfaceStructureSet')">Check answers</button></div>
    <div id="surfaceStructureSetFeedback" class="feedback"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="activity">
    <h3>Checkpoint quiz 3: Structure transfer</h3>
    <div id="checkpoint3"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="btnrow"><button class="ghost" onclick="go(2)">Back</button><button class="primary" onclick="completeSlideAndGo(3,4)">Continue</button></div>
</section>

<section class="slide" data-title="4. Jagged Frontier" id="slide-frontier">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kicker">Module 4</div>
    <h2>The jagged frontier: what GenAI does well and poorly</h2>
    <p>The “jagged frontier” means GenAI performs very well on some tasks while failing on others that look similarly difficult. For law students, this matters because a polished answer is not the same as a reliable answer. GenAI can be excellent at generating practice, offering alternative explanations, and improving readability. It is weaker when asked to determine final legal judgment, resolve jurisdiction-specific nuance, detect subtle contradictions, or verify authority without external checking.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Useful GenAI task</th><th>Risky adjacent task</th><th>Student safeguard</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Generate unlimited practice hypos.</td><td>Tell you which doctrine controls.</td><td>Use the hypo; do your own issue spotting first.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Explain a concept multiple ways.</td><td>Replace casebook/professor-specific doctrine.</td><td>Compare to assigned materials and class notes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Give feedback on your written answer.</td><td>Write the practice answer for you.</td><td>Write first, then request critique.</td></tr>
