<section class="slide" data-title="8. CORRECT Framework" id="slide-correct">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kicker">Module 8</div>
    <h2>The CORRECT framework for student learning</h2>
    <p>CORRECT turns prompting from a vague request into a learning design. The most important move is to restrict GenAI’s helpfulness so that it produces practice, questions, and feedback rather than answers that replace your thinking.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Student purpose</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>C</strong>ontext</td><td>Tell the model what you are studying and what you already know.</td><td>“I’m a 1L studying minimum contacts after International Shoe and World-Wide Volkswagen.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>O</strong>bjective</td><td>State the learning outcome.</td><td>“I want to practice applying the test to new facts.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>R</strong>ole</td><td>Make GenAI a tutor, professor, judge, or critic.</td><td>“Act as a demanding but fair Civil Procedure professor.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>R</strong>estrictions</td><td>Prevent shortcut behavior.</td><td>“Do not tell me the answer. Ask questions until I explain my reasoning.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>E</strong>xpectations</td><td>Specify output format.</td><td>“Give one fact pattern at a time; no analysis until I submit mine.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>C</strong>larification</td><td>Describe the exact task sequence.</td><td>“Start with a borderline fact pattern, then critique my answer.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T</strong>one</td><td>Set the feedback environment.</td><td>“Be direct, exacting, and supportive.”</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
