<section class="slide" data-title="9. Integrity to PR" id="slide-pr">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kicker">Module 9</div>
    <h2>From academic integrity to professional responsibility</h2>
    <p>Academic integrity is professional responsibility in early form. The habits you build while using GenAI in law school—reading rules, asking permission, disclosing assistance, verifying sources, protecting confidential information, and retaining independent judgment—are the same habits lawyers need in practice.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Academic habit</th><th>Professional analogue</th><th>Core risk</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Read each professor’s GenAI policy.</td><td>Read court rules and judge-specific standing orders.</td><td>Assuming rules are uniform when they are not.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Disclose AI assistance when required.</td><td>Comply with court, client, firm, and bar guidance.</td><td>Misrepresentation or lack of candor.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Verify citations and facts.</td><td>Rule 11, candor, competence, and malpractice prevention.</td><td>Fabricated authority or unsupported factual claims.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Do your own analysis.</td><td>Competent representation and supervision of technology.</td><td>Polished but indefensible work product.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Protect exam and clinic confidentiality.</td><td>Client confidentiality and privilege.</td><td>Improper disclosure through prompts or uploads.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>The same 4-D habit in professional practice</h3>
    <p>The habits you practice in school map directly onto lawyer duties. When a professor asks you to disclose GenAI use, verify sources, or follow a course-specific rule, that is not just academic bureaucracy. It is rehearsal for standing orders, client confidentiality, Rule 11, candor to tribunals, and competent supervision of technology.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>4-D move</th><th>In law school</th><th>In practice</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Delegation</strong></td><td>Do not let GenAI do the assigned legal analysis unless the assignment expressly allows that use.</td><td>Do not delegate professional judgment, strategy, or final legal conclusions to a tool.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Description</strong></td><td>Be able to state what tool you used, what task it performed, and whether it affected substance.</td><td>Comply with client, firm, court, and judge-specific disclosure or certification requirements.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Discernment</strong></td><td>Ask whether the output is accurate, biased, too generic, or inconsistent with your course materials.</td><td>Recognize hallucinated authority, incomplete analysis, record contradictions, and biased framing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Diligence</strong></td><td>Read the syllabus, verify sources, protect exam/clinic information, and disclose when required.</td><td>Check standing orders, protect client confidentiality, verify citations, and satisfy Rule 11-style duties.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </div>
